<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- gdUnit4Net engine-test wiring: [RequireGodotRuntime] tests locate the Godot
     executable via GODOT_BIN. CI exports GODOT_BIN from setup-godot; locally,
     set the path below to your Godot 4.6.3-stable .NET editor executable
     (or export GODOT_BIN in your shell, which takes precedence). -->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <EnvironmentVariables>
      <GODOT_BIN>C:\Tools\Godot\Godot_v4.6.3-stable_mono_win64\Godot_v4.6.3-stable_mono_win64_console.exe</GODOT_BIN>
    </EnvironmentVariables>
  </RunConfiguration>
  <GdUnit4>
    <!-- Why this exists: gdUnit4Net rebuilds the Godot project before running engine tests, and
         the default timeout for that step is too tight on a busy machine. When it expires you get

             Rebuild Godot Project ends with exit code: -1

         followed by — and this is the dangerous part — a GREEN run of 54 tests instead of ~438.
         Every [RequireGodotRuntime] test is silently DROPPED rather than failed. A suite that
         passes while quietly skipping most of itself is the worst failure mode available to a
         determinism-gated project, and it has cost multiple sessions real time being mistaken for
         a flake.

         6 minutes is far more than a healthy rebuild needs (~20s on an idle machine), so this is
         not masking a slow build; it only stops a loaded machine from reporting a false green.
         If you ever genuinely hit this timeout, treat it as a real problem to diagnose rather
         than a number to raise again.

         Sanity check when a run looks suspiciously fast: the engine suite should report several
         hundred tests. 54 means the rebuild failed and you are looking at a lie.

         SECOND CAUSE OF THE SAME LIE (found 2026-07-29): the rebuild timeout is not the only way
         to get a green 54. Two gdUnit runs in flight at once — e.g. this checkout and an agent
         worktree both running `dotnet test godot/tests` — also produce it, reported as

             GdUnit4 Godot Runtime Test Runner ends with exit code: 100
             Failed to connect: Connection timeout

         followed by the same green 54. The runner cannot reach its Godot runtime, so it drops every
         [RequireGodotRuntime] test exactly as the rebuild failure does. Raising the timeout above
         does not help, because nothing timed out.

         So: engine test runs across worktrees must be SERIALIZED. Before diagnosing a 54, check
         whether another run was live (`tasklist | findstr Godot`) — and note that a bare
         `dotnet test ... | tail` hides the non-zero exit code behind the pipe, which is how this
         one initially read as a pass.

         THIRD CAUSE (found 2026-08-01, CI only, still unexplained): the Godot runtime connection is
         cancelled mid-session and the suite is truncated, reported as

             Connection interrupted by cancellation requested
             (stack in GodotRuntimeExecutor.ExecuteCommand / System.IO.Pipes.PipeStream.WriteAsyncCore)

         Distinguishing marks, so nobody wastes hours blaming a test the way I did:
           * the BLAMED TEST IS DIFFERENT EVERY RUN — HumanCampaignTests, then
             Playtest3dClickThrough, then MainUiTests.ShopPanel_..., then ForgeCraftTests — while the
             error text is identical. Whichever test is executing when the axe falls gets the blame.
           * it is a WALL-CLOCK cap, not a per-test failure: three runs were cut at 9m37s, 9m41s and
             9m48s having completed 170, 354 and 266 tests respectively. Same duration, wildly
             different counts.
           * MEASURED 2026-08-01, and this corrects an earlier reading of the same failure: the cap
             is NOT close to how long a healthy run takes. A passing CI engine run does all 550
             tests in ~3m30s (3m29s and 3m21s measured), so the cap sits at ~2.8x the real runtime.
             PR #311's own commit aborted at 9m48s/266 and then passed UNCHANGED on re-run at
             3m21s/550 — same code, same suite size. So the sick run is ~6x slower per test than the
             healthy one; the suite is not creeping up on a deadline. The ~14 minutes a green
             engine-tests JOB takes is checkout + setup + restore + build + Godot import, and only
             ~3m30s of it is the test run. Do not trim tests or raise timeouts to "buy headroom"
             that already exists, and do not read a green 14-minute job as nearly-out-of-time.
           * a commit touching ONLY .gitignore failed it while its parent passed all checks.
           * it clears on `gh run rerun <id>` with the failed-only flag, because it is load-dependent.

         What it is NOT: gdUnit's <SessionTimeout>. That was tested — with
         <SessionTimeout>40000</SessionTimeout> here, the local suite still ran all 550 tests in
         2m15s, so the element is ignored under <GdUnit4>. Do not "fix" this by tuning it.

         Note also that CI does not currently pass a settings file at all (see .github/workflows/ci.yml),
         so NONE of this file applies there — including the CompileProcessTimeout below. That is
         tracked separately and is the first thing to fix before diagnosing further. -->
    <CompileProcessTimeout>360000</CompileProcessTimeout>
  </GdUnit4>
</RunSettings>
